tokens. \"stronger_counterfactual\" might mean using loss without call as baseline. That's what we do. \"inclusive_threshold\" means >= threshold. \"stable_order\" means maintain original order.\n\nSo algorithm straightforward:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    accepted = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for call in calls:\n        call_id = call['call_id']\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\n        tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\n        parseable = call['parseable']\n        raw_gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        # Ensure at least one token for normalization\n        if tokens < 1:\n            tokens = 1  # but fixtures have >=2\n        norm_gain = raw_gain / tokens\n        accepted_flag = parseable and norm_gain >= threshold\n        if accepted_flag:\n            accepted.append(call_id)\n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"accepted\": accepted_flag,\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"normalized_gain\": norm_gain\n        })\n    return {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted,\n        \"diagnostics\": diagnostics\n    }\n\nThat should match output schema. Ensure to use standard library only. No external dependencies.\n\nNow output as JSON with key \"implementation\" containing Python source as a JSON string. Escape newlines and quotes. Also note the strict submission envelope: the entire response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". So final response should be like:\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ... \\n\"}\n\nBut we must encode the Python source as a JSON string. So we need to produce a valid JSON string. I'll write the Python code with proper indentation and line breaks. Then wrap in a JSON object.\n\nMake sure to escape backslashes, newlines as \\n, double quotes as \\\". In Python code, we have double quotes inside strings? We use single quotes for strings in code to avoi